export const categoryGuides: Record<string, string> = {
  'fat-burners': `<h2>What “fat burner” actually means</h2><p>“Fat burner” is a marketing category, not one mechanism. Most formulas combine stimulants intended to raise alertness or energy expenditure, fibres intended to affect fullness, and plant extracts promoted for fat metabolism. That combination does not make the finished product clinically proven. The useful question is smaller: which ingredient, at what dose, tested in whom, with what result?</p><h2>How the common approaches work</h2><p>Caffeine stimulates the central nervous system and can temporarily increase alertness and energy expenditure. Appetite-oriented products often use soluble fibres that absorb water. Thermogenic blends may pair caffeine with green tea catechins or pepper extracts. None of these routes replaces an energy deficit, sleep or a sustainable eating pattern, and a mechanistic explanation is not proof of meaningful weight loss.</p><h2>What to look for</h2><p>Start with the total caffeine per full daily serving, including guarana, yerba mate and tea extracts. Reject a proprietary blend that hides stimulant amounts. Check whether the extract is standardised, whether the dose matches the cited research and whether batch testing can be verified. Be sceptical of “detox,” spot-reduction and effortless-loss language.</p><h2>Common ingredients</h2><h3>Caffeine</h3><p><strong>Evidence: modest.</strong> It can increase energy expenditure for a period, but tolerance develops and more is not automatically better. Count every source on the label. Anxiety, insomnia, palpitations and blood-pressure effects matter.</p><h3>Green tea extract</h3><p><strong>Evidence: small or uncertain effect.</strong> NIH’s Office of Dietary Supplements says green tea might help with a small amount of weight loss, while concentrated extract can cause adverse effects and has been linked to liver injury in some people. Tea as a drink and a concentrated extract are not interchangeable.</p><h3>Glucomannan</h3><p><strong>Evidence: weak for weight loss.</strong> This konjac-root fibre absorbs water, but the NIH fact sheet concludes it has little to no effect on weight loss. It can cause gastrointestinal symptoms and must be taken with adequate fluid.</p><h3>Green coffee bean and garcinia</h3><p><strong>Evidence: limited.</strong> Small signals are not a reason to ignore study quality, short duration or stimulant content. A formula should not turn preliminary evidence into a confident promise.</p><p>Primary reference: <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/WeightLoss-Consumer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Weight-Loss Supplements</a>.</p><h2>Who should pause before using one</h2><p>People who are pregnant or breastfeeding, under 18, sensitive to stimulants, preparing for surgery, or managing heart rhythm, blood pressure, anxiety, liver or medication issues should speak with a qualified professional first. Combining a fat burner with coffee, pre-workout products or decongestants can raise the real stimulant exposure beyond the number that caught your eye.</p><h2>Sumita’s editorial note</h2><p>A crowded label does not impress me. A disclosed dose, a restrained claim and evidence that matches the actual formula do. If the product needs a proprietary blend to stop you checking the dose, that is already useful information.</p>`,
  nootropics: `<h2>Nootropics without the science-fiction gloss</h2><p>Nootropic is a loose commercial label for products sold for focus, memory, alertness or stress resilience. It can cover ordinary caffeine, nutrients that correct a deficiency, herbal extracts and compounds with far thinner human evidence. Those are different jobs. A product that makes somebody feel stimulated has not necessarily improved memory or executive function.</p><h2>How cognitive supplements can act</h2><p>Stimulants affect arousal and attention. Nutrients support normal physiology when intake or status is inadequate. Other ingredients are proposed to influence neurotransmission, blood flow or cellular energy, but a plausible pathway does not establish a real-world benefit. Sleep loss, medication effects, iron or B12 deficiency, depression and thyroid problems can all resemble a “focus” problem and deserve proper assessment.</p><h2>What to look for</h2><p>Demand an exact caffeine total and avoid proprietary blends. Match extracts by species, plant part and standardisation. Look for trials measuring a relevant cognitive task rather than only mood or self-reported energy. Check the population: a result in sleep-deprived adults or people with impairment cannot automatically be applied to every healthy user.</p><h2>Common ingredients</h2><h3>Caffeine with L-theanine</h3><p><strong>Evidence: moderate for short-term alertness, narrower for broader cognition.</strong> The practical questions are dose, timing and total caffeine from every source. Sleep lost to a late dose can erase the benefit somebody hoped to gain.</p><h3>Creatine monohydrate</h3><p><strong>Evidence: mixed.</strong> Two 2024 reviews reached cautious conclusions. One meta-analysis of 16 trials reported possible benefits in selected cognitive domains but rated much of the evidence low certainty; another systematic review described the cognitive results as equivocal. That disagreement is exactly why a review should avoid a sweeping “brain booster” claim. See <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39070254/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Xu et al., 2024</a> and <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38582412/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McMorris et al., 2024</a>.</p><h3>Omega-3 fatty acids</h3><p><strong>Evidence: context-dependent.</strong> EPA and DHA are important nutrients, but a fish-oil capsule is not a proven general memory enhancer. Observational links with fish intake do not establish the same effect from supplements.</p><h3>Ginkgo, bacopa and mushroom extracts</h3><p><strong>Evidence: product- and population-specific.</strong> Standardisation, trial length and extract identity matter. “Contains bacopa” is not enough information. Mushroom powders and extracts vary sharply, and marketing has moved faster than robust clinical evidence.</p><h2>Who should be cautious</h2><p>Pregnancy, breastfeeding, childhood, surgery, psychiatric treatment, anticoagulant use, seizure disorders and sensitivity to stimulants all warrant professional advice. A supplement should not be used to mask persistent cognitive change, severe fatigue or a sudden new symptom.</p><h2>Sumita’s editorial note</h2><p>My pet peeve is a focus blend that hides 12 ingredients behind one number. Cognition is difficult to measure well. If the company will not even show the dose, the reader cannot compare the product with the research.</p>`,
  wellness: `<h2>“Wellness” is too broad to be a claim</h2><p>This category includes multivitamins, greens powders, probiotics, sleep formulas, minerals and products for general resilience. The breadth makes label reading more important. A multivitamin and a probiotic do not share a mechanism, evidence base or safety profile. “Supports wellness” says almost nothing until the ingredient, dose and intended outcome are defined.</p><h2>Start with the need</h2><p>A supplement is most defensible when it addresses a plausible gap: a documented deficiency, a life stage with established requirements, a restricted diet or a specific use supported by evidence. More ingredients do not create more insurance. They can create duplication, interactions and doses that are simultaneously too small to help and large enough to complicate the label.</p><h2>What to look for</h2><p>Compare vitamin and mineral amounts with established daily values and tolerable upper limits. Check chemical forms where absorption or tolerability differs. For probiotics, strain identity matters; a species name alone cannot be mapped to a strain-specific trial. For botanicals, look for the plant part, extraction ratio and standardisation. For greens powders, ask what problem the powder is meant to solve and whether the blend discloses enough to test that idea.</p><h2>Common ingredients</h2><h3>Vitamin D</h3><p><strong>Evidence: strong for correcting deficiency, not universal benefit at any dose.</strong> Baseline status, age and clinical context matter. High-dose routines deserve professional oversight rather than guesswork.</p><h3>Magnesium</h3><p><strong>Evidence: depends on the outcome and the person.</strong> Different salts contain different amounts of elemental magnesium and vary in gastrointestinal tolerance. A front label stating the compound weight can be misleading if the Supplement Facts panel does not make the elemental amount clear.</p><h3>Omega-3 fatty acids</h3><p><strong>Evidence: outcome-specific.</strong> Check the actual EPA and DHA amounts, not just “fish oil 1,000 mg.” Food intake, indication, oxidation control and medicine interactions affect the decision.</p><h3>Probiotics</h3><p><strong>Evidence: strain-specific.</strong> A trial on one strain cannot validate every capsule with the same genus. Viable count at the end of shelf life, storage instructions and the exact strain designation are basic checks.</p><h3>Greens and adaptogen blends</h3><p><strong>Evidence: often difficult to map to the finished product.</strong> Proprietary blends, small servings and changing botanical sources make confident conclusions hard. They should not be presented as replacements for fruit, vegetables, protein or medical care.</p><h2>Safety still applies to “natural” products</h2><p>Natural does not mean interaction-free. Pregnancy, breastfeeding, liver or kidney disease, surgery, anticoagulants and multiple medicines change the risk calculation. Check for duplicated nutrients across a multivitamin, fortified foods and single-ingredient products.</p><p>For regulatory context, see the FDA’s <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dietary supplement guidance for consumers</a>.</p><h2>Sumita’s editorial note</h2><p>I would rather see five fully disclosed ingredients with a clear purpose than 40 ingredients sprinkled into a “daily foundation” blend. The label should let you test the idea behind the product. If it cannot, the marketing is doing more work than the formula.</p>`,
};
